course: religion-ethics
lesson: 47
title: The environment
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Christians believe the earth is God's creation, given to people to look after like stewards, who care for something that belongs to someone else; many other faiths and humanists also believe we must care for the earth.
explain: The earth is warming, mainly because of human activity, and this harms people and nature, often those far away or not yet born.
explain: Small habits add up: if a whole city reduces, reuses and recycles, the sea gets less plastic and the air gets cleaner.
explain: Some questions are hard, like who should pay to fix the damage; a good answer weighs fairness, who caused the harm and who is able to help.
explain: For a child today this means switching off lights, walking when you can, sharing things fairly and speaking up for nature politely and bravely.
fact: Christians believe the earth is God's creation and that people are its stewards, called to look after it.
fact: Many other faiths and humanists also believe we have a duty to care for the earth.
fact: The earth is warming mainly because of human activity, such as burning coal, oil and gas.
fact: Climate change harms both people and nature, including people far away and people in the future.
fact: The three Rs are reduce (use less), reuse (use things again) and recycle (turn old materials into new things).
fact: Reducing usually helps most, because what is never made never needs to be thrown away.
fact: Plastic rubbish in the sea harms sea creatures, which can swallow it or get trapped in it.
fact: Future generations are the people who will live after us; fairness includes not using up what they will need.
fact: Whether rich countries that polluted more should pay to help poorer countries is a disputed question.
fact: The tragedy of the commons is when everyone uses a shared thing for themselves until it is ruined for all.
fact: Shared things need shared rules, so that everyone can keep using them.
fact: Walking or cycling instead of taking the car takes longer but harms the air and climate less.
fact: Young people such as Greta Thunberg, a Swedish girl who missed school on Fridays to protest, have spoken up about the environment; people disagree about which ways of protesting are right.
exercise: What does it mean to be a steward of creation? | To look after the earth carefully, as something entrusted to us | To own the earth and use it however we like | To leave all care of the earth to God alone | To protect only the places we ourselves visit
exercise: Why might a Christian say littering is wrong? | It spoils God's good creation, which we are asked to care for | It is wrong only when a policeman is watching | It is wrong only because it makes the street ugly for Christians | It is fine, because God will clean it up anyway
exercise: What is the main cause of the earth warming today? | Human activity, such as burning coal, oil and gas | The Sun moving much closer to the earth each year | Too many animals breathing out warm air | Volcanoes erupting more often than they used to
exercise: What duties do we have to people not yet born? | To leave them a world that is still safe and good to live in | None, because they cannot ask us for anything yet | To save all of our old toys and games for them up in the attic | Only to make sure they get our old houses
exercise: What do the three Rs stand for? | Reduce, reuse, recycle | Read, write, reckon | Rest, relax, repeat | Repair, replace, refuse
exercise: Which of the three Rs usually helps most, and why? | Reduce, because what is never made never becomes rubbish | Recycle, because then we can use as much as we like without worry | Reuse, because new things are always bad things, wherever they come from | None, because one person's habits cannot ever matter to the world
exercise: What could your class do to use less plastic? | Bring lunch in reusable boxes and bottles instead of wrappers | Buy more plastic bags so there are spares | Throw plastic in the river so it gets carried away and out of sight | Wait until scientists invent something to fix it
exercise: Why does plastic in the sea matter to a child living far from the coast? | Rubbish from far away can reach the sea, so our choices affect others | It does not matter at all if you live inland | Plastic in the sea turns into fish food in the end | Only the people who live by the sea make plastic
exercise: Is it fair to use up resources our grandchildren will need? | No, fairness includes people who will live after us | Yes, because they will invent new things anyway | Yes, because we were here first and that settles it | It does not matter, because no one can know the future
exercise: Should rich countries that polluted more help poorer countries pay for the damage? Which answer is best-reasoned? | It is disputed: many say yes, as they caused more harm and can pay; others raise other concerns | Yes, just because rich people are always bad | No, just because poor countries should look after themselves | No one should ever pay, because the weather is nobody's fault
exercise: What is the tragedy of the commons? | When everyone uses a shared thing for themselves until it is ruined for all | When a village field is too small for even one cow | When nobody wants to use something shared | When a play about a village has a sad ending
exercise: The class library keeps losing books because nobody returns them. What does this show? | Shared things need shared rules, like signing books out | Libraries are a bad idea and should all be closed straight away | Only the teacher should be allowed to read the class books | Books should never be lent to anyone, not even to good readers
exercise: When is it worth walking even if it takes longer? | When the trip is short and safe, so you save fuel and clean air | Never, because the car is always quicker and quicker is always better | Only when the car is broken | Only when it is raining very hard
exercise: What is a respectful way for children to speak up for nature? | Write letters, give talks and make posters, being polite and honest | Shout at adults until they give in | Break things in the street so that people notice what you are angry about | Stay silent, because children have no right to speak
exercise: Which statement about Greta Thunberg is fair? | She is a young person who spoke up about the climate; views on her protest methods differ | Everyone in the world agrees with all her methods | She is a scientist who invented recycling | She said children should never talk about nature
exercise: Do other faiths and none care about the earth too? | Yes, many faiths and humanists teach caring for the earth | No, only Christians care about the earth | No, only scientists are allowed to care about it, not religions | No, caring for the earth is against most religions
test: Your cousin says, "Climate change is just the Sun getting closer, so it's not our fault." How do you put it right? | The earth is warming mainly because of human activity | He is right, the Sun gets a little closer to us every summer | He is right, and so nothing at all can be done about it | He is right, and it is really the Moon's fault as well
test: Friend: "recycling's pointless, it's just one can, who cares lol." Which reply is best-reasoned? | One can is small, but millions of people's habits add up | You're stupid, so I won't listen to you | You're right, so I'll throw all my cans away | Recycling is good because the teacher said so, end of story
test: Mum: "Get in the car, the shop's only 5 minutes' walk but I can't be bothered." What is the best thing to suggest? | Walk this time: it is short, healthy and better for the air | Drive, because walking is never good for you, even a little bit | Drive, but leave the engine running at the shop | Stay home and never buy anything again
test: A classmate who is not religious asks, "Why should I care about the planet if I don't believe God made it?" What is a good answer? | It is the only home for all of us and for people after us | You can't care unless you are a Christian | You shouldn't, because the planet is only meant for believers | Because God will punish you if you don't
test: The football field at the park is churned to mud because every team plays on it every day. What is the best idea? | Agree a shared timetable so the grass can recover | Let the strongest team in the park have it all the time | Ban everyone from ever playing football there again | Keep on playing every day and hope it fixes itself
test: "Who cares about people in 100 years, I won't be alive, sod 'em." What is wrong with this argument? | It ignores fairness to people who will live after us | It uses far too many numbers and dates to make its point | It is too kind to future people | Nothing, because only living people count
test: A judge asks: "Is it fair for poor countries to pay as much as rich ones for climate damage?" Which answer is best? | Many say no, as rich countries caused more of it and can afford more, though it is disputed | Yes, everyone must always pay exactly the same | No, poor countries should never do anything at all | Who cares, it's all about money anyway
test: Your sister says, "Reuse means putting it in the recycling bin." How do you correct her? | Reuse means using a thing again, like refilling a bottle | Reuse means going out and buying a new one of the same thing | Reuse means throwing it out and then forgetting all about it | She is right, reuse and recycle mean exactly the same thing
test: A boy in Sydney wants to stop the beach getting more plastic. Which plan is best? | Pick up litter safely and get his family to use fewer plastic things | Bury the plastic in the sand where no one sees it | Throw the plastic out to sea on a windy day | Ask the fish to avoid the plastic
test: "Greta's a total idiot for skipping school." What is weak about this point? | It attacks the person instead of discussing her reasons | It uses lots of facts about the climate that are simply untrue | It is much too polite and careful about what it says of her | It gives far too many reasons on both sides of the argument
test: Which is the best summary of Christian stewardship? | The earth is God's gift and people must care for it well | The earth belongs to people to use up as they wish | The earth does not matter at all, because heaven matters more | Only priests are asked to care for the earth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Christian doctrine of stewardship in respect of the natural world? | Humanity is entrusted with the care of a creation which belongs to God | Humanity is the absolute proprietor of the earth and may dispose of it at will | The care of the earth is the exclusive responsibility of the clergy | The natural world is held to be without moral significance
exam: Identify the principal cause to which the present warming of the earth is attributed. | Human activity, in particular the combustion of coal, oil and gas | A progressive diminution of the distance between the earth and the Sun | The respiration of an increased population of animals | An increase in the frequency of volcanic eruptions
exam: Whereas a common resource is exploited by each party solely for its own advantage, ascertain the outcome which the expression "the tragedy of the commons" denotes. | The resource is degraded to the detriment of all who depend upon it | The resource is enlarged in proportion to the number of its users | The resource is abandoned by all parties at the outset | The resource is transferred to the ownership of the state
exam: Of the three principles of waste reduction, select that which is generally held to afford the greatest benefit, together with the reason. | Reduction, inasmuch as an article never produced requires no disposal | Recycling, inasmuch as it permits unrestricted consumption | Reuse, inasmuch as all newly manufactured articles are harmful to the earth | None, inasmuch as the conduct of individuals is of no consequence
